WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.730
ANU SRIVASTAVA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.490
I'm Anu Srivastava,
and I'm going

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.180
to show you how you can use
Vertex AI to train and serve

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.110
a TensorFlow model using
code in a custom container.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.590
We'll be using a data set of
technical specs of automobiles

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.360
to build a model
that can predict

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:24.000
the fuel efficiency of late
1970s and early 1980s cars.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.400
You can find this
data set on Kaggle.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.860
We must provide the
model with description

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.000
of several automobiles
from that time period.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.810
The description
includes attributes

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.830
like cylinders, displacement,
horsepower, and weight.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.900
We're working mainly
in a notebook,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.300
so think of this as an
interactive web tool

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.870
where you can combine code,
computational output, text,

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.700
and multimedia resources
in a single home.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.660
So we've set up a container in
here, a portable environment

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.130
for working with our model.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:53.700
Don't worry.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.740
We have all the source
code and instructions

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.180
for you in a Codelab to
try this out at home.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.620
So now that everything's set
up in here, let's train the job

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.510
on the platform.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.120
I can go here to
the Trainings tab.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.530
Create a new job.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.170
I'll name my demo, add
a few other settings,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.600
like our container
URL and compute specs,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.670
and now it's off to the races.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.170
This one actually takes
10 to 15 minutes to train,

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.260
so I ran a job ahead of time
so we can test it out together.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.070
So now that it's trained,
we deploy an endpoint.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.140
And we can now use this
back in our notebook.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.200
So now for the moment of truth.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.010
Let's predict a sample.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.860
I've added a cell down
here with a sample case.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.833
So this is a set of attributes
that we described earlier,

00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:38.250
but they've been
normalized, which

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.710
is why they don't look
exactly like, say, a year

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.240
or the weight of a car.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.840
I hit Run, and we can see that
an automobile with these values

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.110
would give us approximately
16 miles per gallon.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.200
So to make this
visual, I can use

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.030
a lot of ready-to-go
functionality

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.950
to visualize my data down here.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.560
Use graphs and charts
to share your findings

00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:59.790
with your friends.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.120
We hope we've inspired you to
try working with your own data.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.880
Or to recreate this demo,
check out the links provided.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

